X	application-support	p	**No, not as a whole.** Application Support is where apps keep their own data: libraries, projects, chat history, downloaded content and settings too big for a preferences file. Deleting an app’s folder resets that app and can lose what you made in it.
X	application-support	p	**What you can remove:** the folders of apps you’ve already deleted. Moving an app to the Trash leaves its folder here, and once the app is gone for good, that folder is a leftover.
X	application-support	p	**Does it come back?** Only as an empty folder. An app creates a fresh one the next time it runs, but the old contents are gone.
X	application-support	h	Measure it
X	application-support	code	du -sh ~/Library/'Application Support'/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -15
X	application-support	h	How to clear it
X	application-support	li	1. Look for folders whose app you no longer have. Match each name to an app: `com.spotify.client` is Spotify, and a plain name is usually the app’s own.
X	application-support	li	2. Check the app really is gone, from Applications and from `~/Applications`, and that nothing else from the same maker uses the folder. The `Google` folder, for example, holds data for Chrome and Google’s other apps.
X	application-support	li	3. If there might be something of yours inside, such as exports or saved games, copy it out first.
X	application-support	li	4. Move the folder to the Trash, and empty the Trash once you’re sure.
X	application-support	p	For apps you still use, don’t delete their folder to save space. Remove downloads, old projects or offline content in the app’s own settings instead. To remove an app along with what it keeps, use its uninstaller if it has one: Apple calls that the best way to remove an app and its related data.
X	application-support	h	Sources
X	application-support	src	Apple: macOS Library Directory Details (Application Support): https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/FileManagement/Conceptual/FileSystemProgrammingGuide/MacOSXDirectories/MacOSXDirectories.html
