X	application-scripts	p	**No point.** The folder holds hundreds of subfolders, but almost all of them are empty. On the Mac this page was checked on, 722 of them added up to zero bytes.
X	application-scripts	p	**What it costs you:** any script you or an app’s setup placed there would stop working, and the app feature that ran it with it.
X	application-scripts	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. macOS makes an app’s folder again when the app next asks for it.
X	application-scripts	h	Measure it
X	application-scripts	code	du -sh ~/Library/Application\ Scripts
X	application-scripts	code	find ~/Library/Application\ Scripts -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 2 | head -20
X	application-scripts	h	How to clear it
X	application-scripts	p	This folder turns up in clean-up advice because it looks cluttered, with a folder for every app you have ever opened, including ones long since removed. But clutter in a hidden folder costs nothing, and there are two small risks in clearing it:
X	application-scripts	li	- A script inside one of the folders is something you, or an app’s installer, put there on purpose. Remove it and the feature that ran it stops, often without an error.
X	application-scripts	li	- Apps expect their folder to exist. macOS recreates it, but only when the app asks.
X	application-scripts	p	If what you want is to remove the traces of an app you’ve uninstalled, its empty folder here is the least of them. The app’s data is in `Containers`, `Group Containers` and `Application Support`: see how to delete apps completely. If you do tidy an old app’s folder here, run the `find` command first and move only that one folder to the Trash.
