X	application-scripts	src	Apple Developer: FileManager.SearchPathDirectory.applicationScriptsDirectory: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/filemanager/searchpathdirectory/applicationscriptsdirectory
X	biome	p	**Leave it.** Biome is the store macOS uses to build suggestions from how you use your Mac. It is normally around 150 MB, and its own background services keep it in order.
X	biome	p	**What it costs you:** nothing you made is in there, so clearing it shouldn’t lose data, but macOS forgets the patterns its suggestions are based on and starts collecting again.
X	biome	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. The Biome services recreate what they need while you work.
X	biome	h	Measure it
X	biome	code	du -sh ~/Library/Biome
X	biome	code	du -sh ~/Library/Biome/* | sort -h
X	biome	h	How to clear it
X	biome	p	At its usual size there’s nothing to gain. Apple gives no setting for its size and no button to clear it, and the files are in use by services that run all the time. If yours is far larger than normal, work up from the gentlest step:
X	biome	li	1. **Restart the Mac.** Oakley reports that Biome is robust and that runaway processor use by its services is usually put right by a restart.
X	biome	li	2. **Install any waiting macOS update**, in case the fault has been fixed since.
X	biome	li	3. **Measure again after a day or two** with the `du` lines above, to see whether it is still growing.
X	biome	li	4. **Only as a last resort**, with a current backup, remove what is inside `~/Library/Biome` and restart. Oakley’s view is that deleting the folder’s contents shouldn’t result in data loss. Keep the `Biome` folder itself.
X	biome	p	Starting up in Safe Mode doesn’t shrink it. Oakley points out that Biome’s folders are maintained by Biome and aren’t treated as a cache.
X	biome	h	Sources
X	biome	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: Biome isn’t about biometrics, but suggestions: https://eclecticlight.co/2022/06/27/biome-isnt-about-biometrics-but-suggestions/
X	core-spotlight-metadata	p	**No.** This folder is the index that lets Spotlight find things inside your apps, such as a message in Mail or a line in Notes. Apps add their own entries to it, and Apple documents no way to clear it.
X	core-spotlight-metadata	p	**What it costs you:** searches for app content come up empty or incomplete until every app has added its items again, and some may not do that until they’re next opened or updated.
X	core-spotlight-metadata	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, but piece by piece and on each app’s schedule, with the indexing process working hard meanwhile.
X	core-spotlight-metadata	h	Measure it
X	core-spotlight-metadata	code	du -sh ~/Library/Metadata/CoreSpotlight
X	core-spotlight-metadata	code	du -sh ~/Library/Metadata/CoreSpotlight/* | sort -h | tail -5
X	core-spotlight-metadata	h	How to clear it
X	core-spotlight-metadata	p	The trouble with deleting this index is that macOS can’t rebuild it alone. For the file index, Spotlight reads the disk again. Here, each app has to hand its items over again, and nothing tells you when all of them have. Apple’s steps for rebuilding Spotlight, adding a disk or folder to Spotlight’s privacy list and removing it again, apply to the file index, and Apple describes no equivalent for this one.
X	core-spotlight-metadata	p	If the folder is large or `corespotlightd` is busy, these are the safer moves:
X	core-spotlight-metadata	li	1. **Wait out a backlog.** After a macOS upgrade, a move to a new Mac, or a big mail download, the index is being filled. Leave the Mac on and plugged in.
X	core-spotlight-metadata	li	2. **Find the app.** If the growth began after installing or updating something, update that app again or look in its settings for a Spotlight or search option.
X	core-spotlight-metadata	li	3. **Quit `corespotlightd` in Activity Monitor** if its memory stays high for days. macOS starts a fresh copy, and the index is kept.
X	core-spotlight-metadata	li	4. **Remove an app you don’t use.** Its entries are that app’s to maintain, and they have no purpose once it has gone.
X	core-spotlight-metadata	h	Sources
X	core-spotlight-metadata	src	Apple Developer: Core Spotlight: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/corespotlight
X	core-spotlight-metadata	src	Apple: About Spotlight indexing and search results on Apple devices: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102321
X	diagnostic-reports	p	**Yes.** Every file in it is a report macOS wrote after something crashed, hung or ran into trouble. Once you’ve read it, or sent it to whoever needs it, nothing on your Mac uses it.
X	diagnostic-reports	p	**What it costs you:** evidence. If an app keeps crashing and its developer asks for a report, a recent one helps, so keep those until the problem is fixed.
