X	fseventsd	p	**No, leave it.** `.fseventsd` is macOS’s running record of which folders changed on a disk. Backup and sync tools use it to catch up on changes without rescanning everything.
X	fseventsd	p	**What deleting it costs:** the tools that rely on that history lose it, and may have to scan the whole disk to work out what changed. Apple tells developers never to work with these files directly.
X	fseventsd	p	**Does it come back?** At once: macOS’s `fseventsd` service keeps writing it as files change.
X	fseventsd	h	Measure it
X	fseventsd	code	sudo du -sh /System/Volumes/Data/.fseventsd
X	fseventsd	h	How to clear it
X	fseventsd	p	On your Mac’s own disk, leave it. Deleting it gains little and throws away the change history that backup and sync apps depend on. Apple even tells developers not to purge event history, because no app can assume it’s the only one using it.
X	fseventsd	p	On an external drive it does no harm either: other computers ignore it, and a Mac creates it again. Apple’s old developer guide describes switching logging off for a volume with an empty file named `no_log` inside `.fseventsd`, meant for cases like backup volumes. An Apple engineer has since said he wouldn’t recommend opting general-purpose volumes out, and a developer reported that it no longer seemed to work on an APFS volume in macOS 15.5.
X	fseventsd	h	Sources
