X	containers	p	**No, not the folder, and not the container of an app you use.** Each container holds everything one sandboxed app keeps: its documents, settings, caches and databases. Deleting one is like reinstalling that app from scratch, and anything saved only inside it is lost.
X	containers	p	**The exception:** the container of an app you’ve deleted and won’t use again. Removing an app doesn’t remove its container, so that one is a leftover, once you’ve checked nothing in it is yours.
X	containers	p	**Does it come back?** As a fresh, empty container the next time the app opens; the old contents don’t.
X	containers	h	Measure it
X	containers	code	du -sh ~/Library/Containers/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -10
X	containers	h	How to clear it
X	containers	p	For an app you use, free space from inside the app rather than deleting its container: remove downloads, old projects or offline content in its settings, and for Docker, use `docker system prune` instead of touching its files.
X	containers	p	For an app you’ve removed:
X	containers	li	1. Make sure the app is really gone: not in Applications, not in `~/Applications`, and not about to be reinstalled from the App Store.
X	containers	li	2. Open its container in Finder and look in `Data/Documents` and `Data/Library/Application Support` for anything of yours, such as exports or saved files. Copy out what you want to keep.
X	containers	li	3. Move the container to the Trash; macOS may ask for permission first. Empty the Trash when you’re sure.
X	containers	p	If the app has an uninstaller or its maker publishes removal steps, follow those instead: they name the exact folders. Leave the Containers folder itself alone, and Apple’s own `com.apple.…` containers, such as Mail’s.
