X	group-containers	p	**No.** Each folder here holds data that several apps from one developer share, or that an app shares with its widgets and extensions. Deleting one can wipe data more than one app depends on, from Outlook’s mail to the notes in Apple’s Notes.
X	group-containers	p	**When removing one is right:** after you’ve removed every app that shares it, ideally by following the maker’s own uninstall steps, which name the exact folders.
X	group-containers	p	**Does it come back?** As an empty folder if any app in the group runs again; what was in it doesn’t.
X	group-containers	h	Measure it
X	group-containers	code	du -sh ~/Library/'Group Containers'/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -10
X	group-containers	h	How to clear it
X	group-containers	li	1. Remove every app in the group first. For Microsoft Office that means Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook and OneNote, not just one of them.
X	group-containers	li	2. Back up the folder if it might hold something you’ll want, such as mail kept only on this Mac.
X	group-containers	li	3. Follow the maker’s removal steps where they exist. Microsoft’s list `UBF8T346G9.ms`, `UBF8T346G9.Office` and `UBF8T346G9.OfficeOsfWebHost`, and warn that Outlook data is removed with them.
X	group-containers	li	4. Move the folders to the Trash in Finder, then empty it.
X	group-containers	p	Leave every `group.com.apple.…` folder alone. They belong to Apple’s own apps, many of which can’t be removed at all, so their folders are never leftovers.
X	group-containers	h	Sources
X	group-containers	src	Apple: Accessing app group containers in your existing macOS app: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/accessing-app-group-containers
X	group-containers	src	Apple: Configuring app groups: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/xcode/configuring-app-groups
X	group-containers	src	Microsoft: Uninstall Office for Mac: https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/uninstall-office-for-mac-eefa1199-5b58-43af-8a3d-b73dc1a8cae3
X	library-updates	p	**No.** `/Library/Updates` belongs to Software Update. macOS marks it as protected, so it can’t be emptied in Finder or with `sudo` while System Integrity Protection is on.
X	library-updates	p	**What frees the space instead:** installing the updates that are waiting. Packages here are downloads on their way to being installed.
X	library-updates	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. Software Update rewrites its catalogue here each time it checks, and fetches the next update when there is one.
X	library-updates	h	Measure it
X	library-updates	code	du -sh /Library/Updates
X	library-updates	code	ls -la /Library/Updates
X	library-updates	code	softwareupdate --list
X	library-updates	h	How to clear it
X	library-updates	p	Because of the `restricted` flag, removing these files isn’t possible in normal use, and the ways around that, starting from Recovery or switching off System Integrity Protection, cost far more than the folder is worth. The supported way to empty it is to let the updates through:
X	library-updates	li	1. **Open System Settings › General › Software Update.** Apple’s steps are to let it check, then click the button to update or restart.
X	library-updates	li	2. **Install what’s offered**, the smaller updates such as Safari’s as well as macOS itself.
X	library-updates	li	3. **Restart if asked**, and keep the Mac awake until it finishes.
X	library-updates	li	4. **Check the folder again** with the `du` line above.
X	library-updates	p	If an update refuses to install because the disk is full, free room elsewhere first: see not enough space to update macOS.
X	library-updates	h	Sources
X	library-updates	src	Apple: Update macOS on Mac: https://support.apple.com/en-us/108382
X	library-updates	src	Apple: Keep your Mac up to date: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mchlpx1065/mac
X	library-updates	src	macOS manual page: softwareupdate(8): https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/softwareupdate.8.html
X	swap-files	p	**No.** Swap files hold parts of memory macOS moved to the disk to make room. They’re in use for as long as your Mac runs, and macOS creates and removes them itself as memory demand changes.
X	swap-files	p	**What to do instead:** close what’s using the memory. Quitting heavy apps brings swap use down, and after a restart the Mac starts with none in use.
X	swap-files	p	**Does it come back?** Whenever memory runs short again. A lot of swap is a sign the Mac needs more memory for what you ask of it, not a cleaning problem.
X	swap-files	h	Measure it
X	swap-files	code	sysctl vm.swapusage
X	swap-files	h	How to clear it
X	swap-files	p	The files belong to the running system, and removing one would pull memory out from under running apps. macOS offers no way to delete them, and you don’t need one:
X	swap-files	li	1. Open Activity Monitor, click Memory, and sort by the Memory column to see which apps use the most.
X	swap-files	li	2. Quit what you don’t need: browsers with many tabs, virtual machines, large editors.
X	swap-files	li	3. Restart if swap stays high. The Mac starts with fresh memory and nothing in swap.
X	swap-files	li	4. If Memory Pressure is often yellow or red, the Mac could use more memory: Apple says yellow means it might eventually need more RAM, and red that it needs more. System Information shows whether yours can be upgraded; on Macs with Apple silicon it can’t, so it’s a choice for your next Mac.
X	swap-files	h	Sources
X	swap-files	src	Apple: View memory usage in Activity Monitor on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/activity-monitor/view-memory-usage-actmntr1004/mac
X	swap-files	src	Apple: Check if your Mac needs more RAM: https://support.apple.com/kb/PH19650
X	swap-files	src	Apple Platform Security: Role of Apple File System (the VM volume): https://support.apple.com/guide/security/seca6147599e/web
X	private-var-log	p	**No need.** `/private/var/log` belongs to macOS, which rotates and compresses these logs by itself. On most Macs the whole folder is tens of megabytes, so there is little space to win.
X	private-var-log	p	**What it costs you:** the record of installs, updates and disk checks that you or a technician would read when something goes wrong.
X	private-var-log	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. System services write to these files all day and would start new ones straight away.
X	private-var-log	h	Measure it
X	private-var-log	code	du -sh /private/var/log
X	private-var-log	code	du -sh /private/var/log/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -8
X	private-var-log	h	How to clear it
X	private-var-log	p	macOS already keeps this folder in check, in two ways you can see on your own Mac:
X	private-var-log	li	- `newsyslog` archives log files when they pass a set size or age and keeps a fixed number of old copies, optionally compressed. Its settings are in `/etc/newsyslog.conf` and `/etc/newsyslog.d/`. That is why you see `wifi.log.0.bz2`, `wifi.log.1.bz2` and so on, up to a limit.
X	private-var-log	li	- `aslmanager` looks after `system.log` and the `asl` folder using the rules in `/etc/asl.conf`, which rotate and compress `system.log` and cap its total size.
X	private-var-log	p	Removing files by hand means using administrator rights on a system folder while services have those files open. A service can keep writing to a file that no longer has a name, or stop logging until it restarts. For a folder this small, that is risk for no gain.
X	private-var-log	p	If you want space back, look at what’s yours: old logs in your own Library, crash reports and the larger items in System Data.
X	private-var-log	h	Sources
X	private-var-log	src	Apple: View reports in Console on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/console/cnsl664be99a/mac
X	private-var-log	src	macOS manual page: newsyslog(8): https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/newsyslog.8.html
X	private-var-log	src	macOS manual page: hier(7), layout of the file system: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/hier.7.html
X	system-library-caches	p	**No, leave it to macOS.** This is the cache folder shared by every account on the Mac and by background services, and much of it belongs to macOS itself. Parts of it are guarded, so you couldn’t clear everything even if you tried.
X	system-library-caches	p	**What clearing it by hand costs:** an administrator password, for little space. The supported reset for system caches is a safe-mode startup, which clears some of them and lets macOS make them again.
X	system-library-caches	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. Whatever is cleared is rebuilt as it’s needed, which is the other reason clearing it rarely pays.
X	system-library-caches	h	Measure it
X	system-library-caches	code	sudo du -sh /Library/Caches/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -10
X	system-library-caches	h	How to clear it
X	system-library-caches	p	For macOS’s own caches, use Apple’s way: start up in safe mode once, then restart normally. Apple’s support article says safe mode clears some system caches, including font caches and the kernel cache, which are created again automatically as needed.
X	system-library-caches	li	1. On a Mac with Apple silicon: shut down, then press and hold the power button until the startup options appear. Select your startup disk, hold Shift and click Continue in Safe Mode.
X	system-library-caches	li	2. On an Intel Mac: turn it on or restart, and hold Shift until the login window appears.
X	system-library-caches	li	3. Log in. “Safe Boot” shows in the menu bar of the login window. Then restart normally.
X	system-library-caches	p	For a third-party app’s folder that has grown large, use the app’s own settings or uninstaller first. If the app is already gone, move its folder from `/Library/Caches` to the Trash in Finder and enter your administrator password when asked. Leave Apple’s folders alone, and don’t use `sudo rm` here.
X	system-library-caches	h	Sources
X	system-library-caches	src	Apple: Start up your Mac in safe mode: https://support.apple.com/en-us/116946
X	system-library-caches	src	Apple Mac User Guide: Start up your Mac in safe mode: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mh21245/mac
X	var-folders	p	**No, not by hand.** It holds every account’s temporary folder and a second cache folder, both in use right now by running apps and macOS services. Deleting from it underneath them can make them fail until you restart.
X	var-folders	p	**What to do instead:** let macOS clear it. Apple documents that temporary files untouched for three days may be removed by the system, and that the cache folder is emptied when the Mac starts up in safe mode.
X	var-folders	p	**Does it come back?** All the time: everything that runs uses it, so it’s never empty, and isn’t meant to be.
X	var-folders	h	Measure it
X	var-folders	code	du -sh $TMPDIR $(getconf DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR)
X	var-folders	h	How to clear it
X	var-folders	li	1. Restart. Apps that were running let go of their temporary files, and macOS’s cleanup runs as the Mac starts up.
X	var-folders	li	2. If one app’s folder in `T` or `C` is huge, quit that app, then move just that folder, or those files, to the Trash. The name usually says which app it is.
X	var-folders	li	3. To empty the cache folder, start up in safe mode once, then restart normally.
X	var-folders	p	Never run `rm -rf` on `/private/var/folders`, with or without `sudo`. It deletes files that background services have open, along with other accounts’ files.
X	var-folders	h	Sources
X	var-folders	src	confstr(3) manual page: the per-user temporary and cache folders: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/confstr.3.html
X	var-folders	src	Apple Developer Forums: when /tmp and $TMPDIR are cleaned up: https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/71382
X	var-folders	src	Apple Mac User Guide: Start up your Mac in safe mode: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mh21245/mac
X	private-tmp	p	**Leave them.** The folders themselves must exist, and what’s inside often belongs to something that is running right now. macOS clears old files from `/tmp` every day without your help.
X	private-tmp	p	**What it costs you:** emptying them by hand can break an app, an installer or a Terminal session that is mid-task.
X	private-tmp	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. Apps and system services put new temporary files there as soon as they need them.
X	private-tmp	h	Measure it
X	private-tmp	code	du -sh /private/tmp /private/var/tmp 2>/dev/null
X	private-tmp	code	du -sh /private/tmp/* /private/var/tmp/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -8
X	private-tmp	h	How to clear it
X	private-tmp	p	macOS has a cleaner for this. Its manual page, `tmp_cleaner`, says it checks the `/tmp` directory every day for content that hasn’t been modified recently and removes it, and that it first appeared in macOS 14. On macOS 15 the script it runs is set to three days, and it is scheduled by the system; the manual says not to launch it by hand. An Apple engineer described the same three-day rule for earlier versions of macOS, and added that these details aren’t promised and can change between releases.
X	private-tmp	p	`/var/tmp` isn’t in that cleaner’s list, which fits its description as the place for temporary files that should outlast a restart. Each tool is expected to remove what it put there.
X	private-tmp	li	1. **Wait three days** if the space is in `/tmp` and you aren’t in a hurry. Idle files go by themselves.
X	private-tmp	li	2. **Quit the tool that made a large item**, then check again. Many tools delete their temporary files on exit.
X	private-tmp	li	3. **Remove only a file that you created** and know is finished with, for example a download you unpacked there yourself in Terminal. Leave everything owned by `root` or by an account whose name begins with an underscore.
X	private-tmp	li	4. **Never remove the folders themselves**, and don’t run a blanket `rm` across them. Sockets and lock files inside are in use even when they are zero bytes.
X	private-tmp	h	Sources
X	private-tmp	src	macOS manual page: hier(7), layout of the file system: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/hier.7.html
X	private-tmp	src	Apple Developer Forums: When are /tmp and $TMPDIR cleaned up in macOS? (answer from an Apple engineer): https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/71382
X	dyld-cache	p	**No.** The dyld shared cache is where macOS keeps all of its system libraries, the code every app on your Mac runs on. Since macOS 11 those libraries no longer exist as separate files, so this cache is the only copy.
X	dyld-cache	p	**What deleting it would cost:** apps, and macOS itself, couldn’t start. That’s why it lives in parts of the system that are sealed and checked, where you can’t change it.
X	dyld-cache	p	**Does it come back?** It’s replaced with each macOS update and never needs cleaning by hand. There’s nothing to win here.
X	dyld-cache	h	Measure it
X	dyld-cache	code	du -sh /System/Volumes/Preboot/Cryptexes/OS/System/Library/dyld
X	dyld-cache	h	How to clear it
X	dyld-cache	p	There’s no safe way to remove it, and on current macOS no way at all with the Mac running: the system volume is sealed, and the cryptex is verified before it’s mounted. Don’t look for workarounds such as turning off System Integrity Protection.
X	dyld-cache	p	If a disk map showed it to you as something large:
X	dyld-cache	li	- Count it as part of macOS, like the rest of the system. Its size is set by Apple and changes with updates.
X	dyld-cache	li	- Get space back from what’s yours instead: System Data, app caches and old macOS installers.
X	dyld-cache	h	Sources
X	dyld-cache	src	Apple: macOS Big Sur 11.0.1 Release Notes (the built-in dynamic linker cache): https://developer.apple.com/documentation/macos-release-notes/macos-big-sur-11_0_1-release-notes
X	dyld-cache	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: How Cryptexes are changing macOS Ventura: https://eclecticlight.co/2023/04/05/how-cryptexes-are-changing-macos-ventura/
X	dyld-cache	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: Which cryptexes does macOS Tahoe load?: https://eclecticlight.co/2026/01/19/which-cryptexes-does-macos-tahoe-load/
X	dyld-cache	src	WebKit bug 231092: the shared cache moved from /private/var/db/dyld to /System/Library/dyld: https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=231092
X	sleepimage	p	**No, leave it.** It’s where macOS saves a copy of memory so a sleeping Mac can pick up where it was, even if the battery runs out. macOS writes it again the next time the Mac sleeps, so deleting it wins nothing for long.
X	sleepimage	p	**What losing it would cost:** protection for your open work if power fails while the Mac sleeps. The only real way to be rid of it is a power setting, and Apple’s documentation says to use caution with it.
X	sleepimage	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, by itself. On a laptop it’s part of normal sleep.
X	sleepimage	h	Measure it
X	sleepimage	code	ls -lh /private/var/vm/
X	sleepimage	code	pmset -g | grep -E 'hibernate|standby'
X	sleepimage	h	How to clear it
X	sleepimage	p	Removing the file would take `sudo rm` on a system file, and it gains nothing: macOS writes a new one the next time the Mac sleeps. Don’t.
X	sleepimage	p	If you really want no sleep image, it’s the setting that matters, not the file. Apple’s `pmset` manual says hibernation images stop only when `hibernatemode`, `standby` and `autopoweroff` are all 0, and marks the hibernation settings “Please use caution”. On a laptop, that means a battery running flat during sleep takes your open work with it, so SiftMac’s advice is to keep the defaults.
X	sleepimage	p	To free space that stays free, look at what you control: Time Machine’s local snapshots, old macOS installers and the rest of System Data.
X	sleepimage	h	Sources
X	sleepimage	src	pmset(1) manual page: hibernatemode, hibernatefile and standby: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/pmset.1.html
X	spotlight-index	p	**Not by hand.** `.Spotlight-V100` is one disk’s search index, the thing Spotlight, Finder’s search, Mail’s search and many apps look things up in. If it’s huge or broken, have Spotlight rebuild it; on an external drive you never search, turn indexing off instead.
X	spotlight-index	p	**What a rebuild costs you:** searches come up short for a while. Apple says indexing can take hours, or even days, depending on how much is on the disk.
X	spotlight-index	p	**Does it come back?** Yes: Spotlight indexes the disk again, and the index grows back to fit what’s on it.
X	spotlight-index	h	Measure it
X	spotlight-index	code	sudo du -sh /System/Volumes/Data/.Spotlight-V100
X	spotlight-index	code	mdutil -s /
X	spotlight-index	h	How to clear it
X	spotlight-index	p	Apple’s way to rebuild the index of a disk or folder:
X	spotlight-index	li	1. Open System Settings › Spotlight, or Siri & Spotlight on older versions of macOS.
X	spotlight-index	li	2. Click Search Privacy, or Spotlight Privacy.
X	spotlight-index	li	3. Add the disk or folder to the list, wait a few seconds, then select it and remove it again.
X	spotlight-index	li	4. Click Done. Spotlight indexes it again from the start.
X	spotlight-index	p	In Terminal, this erases the startup disk’s index so that Spotlight rebuilds it:
X	spotlight-index	code	sudo mdutil -E /
X	spotlight-index	p	For an external drive you never search, turn indexing off for it so the index doesn’t grow back, then remove the old one with Spotlight’s own tool rather than Finder (put the drive’s name in):
X	spotlight-index	code	sudo mdutil -i off /Volumes/DriveName
X	spotlight-index	code	sudo mdutil -X /Volumes/DriveName
X	spotlight-index	p	To keep the index smaller, add folders you never search, such as virtual machines or build folders, to Search Privacy; Spotlight then leaves them out.
X	spotlight-index	h	Sources
X	spotlight-index	src	Apple: Rebuild the Spotlight index on your Mac: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102321
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	p	**No.** These two folders are the unified log, the record that Console and the `log` command read. A system service called `logd` owns them and prunes them continuously.
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	p	**What it costs you:** the history needed to work out why something crashed or slowed down. The two folders also depend on each other, so removing files from one can leave entries in the other unreadable.
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. `logd` writes new entries every second, and the store grows back to about the same size within days.
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	h	Measure it
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	code	du -sh /private/var/db/diagnostics /private/var/db/uuidtext
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	code	log stats --overview
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	h	How to clear it
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	p	Don’t remove files from these folders in Finder or with `rm`. `logd` has them open, the entries in `diagnostics` refer to text in `uuidtext`, and the store is back to its usual size soon after. You would give up your Mac’s recent history for space you get to use for a few days.
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	p	macOS does provide a command for the rare case where a log really must be cleared, for example before handing a Mac to someone else. The `log` manual page lists `log erase`, which deletes the main log data store, and `log erase --all`, which also deletes the fault and error content. It needs an administrator, and there is no undo.
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	p	If the folders seem unusually large, the better fix is the one Oakley recommends: find what is flooding the log and deal with that. `log stats` names the busiest processes; an app that logs thousands of lines a minute is worth updating or reporting to its maker. See also what `logd` is doing when it’s busy.
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	h	Sources
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: Inside the Unified Log 3, log storage and attrition: https://eclecticlight.co/2025/09/29/inside-the-unified-log-3-log-storage-and-attrition/
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	src	macOS manual page: log(1): https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/log.1.html
X	diagnostics-and-uuidtext	src	macOS manual page: hier(7), layout of the file system: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/hier.7.html
X	library-caches	p	**Yes**, app by app, with each app quit. Everything in it is a copy an app can make again: images and web content it already loaded, compiled code, package archives. Your documents, settings and passwords are kept elsewhere.
X	library-caches	p	**What it costs you:** a little speed for a while. Apps open and load more slowly until they’ve rebuilt what they use, and package managers download again what they need.
X	library-caches	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. It starts refilling as soon as you use your apps, so clearing it buys space for a while rather than for good.
X	library-caches	h	Measure it
X	library-caches	code	du -sh ~/Library/Caches
X	library-caches	code	du -sh ~/Library/Caches/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -12
X	library-caches	h	How to clear it
X	library-caches	li	1. Find the big folders with the command above and work out which app each belongs to. The name usually tells you: `com.google.Chrome` is Chrome, `Homebrew` is Homebrew.
X	library-caches	li	2. Quit that app with `⌘Q`, so nothing is writing to its cache while you clear it.
X	library-caches	li	3. In Finder, open the app’s folder, select everything inside and move it to the Trash. Keep the folder itself, then empty the Trash.
X	library-caches	p	The same in Terminal for one app, with it quit (put the real folder name in):
X	library-caches	code	rm -rf ~/Library/Caches/com.example.app/*
X	library-caches	p	Where a tool has a clean command of its own, use it instead, because it knows what it can drop: `brew cleanup` for Homebrew, `pip cache purge` for pip, `yarn cache clean` for Yarn. Don’t delete `~/Library/Caches` itself, and don’t empty everything at once while apps are open.
X	library-caches	h	Sources
X	library-caches	src	Apple: macOS Library Directory Details (File System Programming Guide): https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/FileManagement/Conceptual/FileSystemProgrammingGuide/MacOSXDirectories/MacOSXDirectories.html
X	dot-underscore-files	p	**Yes**, on a drive that goes into a TV, car stereo, camera or Windows PC. A `._` file holds only Mac extras for the file of the same name, such as Finder tags and the quarantine flag, never the file’s contents.
X	dot-underscore-files	p	**What it costs you:** those extras, for the files whose `._` partner you remove. For music, photos and videos that’s nothing you’d miss; very old Mac files that keep data in a resource fork can stop working.
X	dot-underscore-files	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, whenever a Mac copies or changes files on that drive again. `dot_clean`, built into macOS, clears them.
X	dot-underscore-files	h	Measure it
X	dot-underscore-files	code	find /Volumes/MyDrive -name '._*' -type f | wc -l
X	dot-underscore-files	code	find /Volumes/MyDrive -name '._*' -type f -exec du -k {} + | awk '{s+=$1} END {print s " KB"}'
X	dot-underscore-files	h	How to clear it
X	dot-underscore-files	p	`dot_clean` comes with macOS. With the drive connected:
X	dot-underscore-files	li	1. Find the drive’s name in Finder’s sidebar. In Terminal it’s `/Volumes/` followed by that name.
X	dot-underscore-files	li	2. To remove every `._` file on it, run `dot_clean -m` on the drive. Its manual says `-m` always deletes dot-underscore files.
X	dot-underscore-files	li	3. To remove only orphans, the `._` files whose real file was deleted on a TV or a PC, use `-n` instead: it deletes a dot-underscore file only when there’s no matching file.
X	dot-underscore-files	li	4. Eject the drive in Finder before unplugging it.
X	dot-underscore-files	code	dot_clean -m /Volumes/MyDrive
X	dot-underscore-files	p	Run with no option, `dot_clean` merges each `._` file’s information into its partner file; `-m` also deletes the `._` files, which is what a drive for other devices needs. A zip file’s `__MACOSX` folder can simply go to the Trash after unzipping.
X	dot-underscore-files	h	Sources
X	dot-underscore-files	src	dot_clean(1) man page: merge ._* files with corresponding native files: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/dot_clean.1.html
X	dot-underscore-files	src	Apple Developer Forums: Extended Attributes and Zip Archives (Apple DTS on AppleDouble): https://developer.apple.com/forums/thread/690457
X	dot-underscore-files	src	Wikipedia: AppleSingle and AppleDouble formats: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AppleSingle_and_AppleDouble_formats
X	ds-store-files	p	**Yes.** A `.DS_Store` file only remembers how Finder shows its folder: the view, where icons sit and similar settings. None of your files are inside it.
X	ds-store-files	p	**What it costs you:** that folder opens with Finder’s default view until you arrange it again. Each file is a few kilobytes, so there’s no real space to win.
X	ds-store-files	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. Finder writes a new one when it opens the folder again, so clearing them is mainly for folders you share.
X	ds-store-files	h	Measure it
X	ds-store-files	code	find ~ -name .DS_Store -type f 2>/dev/null | wc -l
X	ds-store-files	code	find ~ -name .DS_Store -type f -exec du -k {} + 2>/dev/null | awk '{s+=$1} END {print s " KB"}'
X	ds-store-files	h	How to clear it
X	ds-store-files	p	On your own Mac there’s no reason to hunt them down. Clear them from a folder you’re about to share, a drive that goes into another device, or a code repository:
X	ds-store-files	li	1. **One folder and everything in it:** in Terminal, run the command below with your folder’s path in place of the example. It removes only files named exactly `.DS_Store`.
X	ds-store-files	li	2. **A USB drive or SD card:** close any Finder window showing it, run the same command on `/Volumes/` followed by the drive’s name, then eject it.
X	ds-store-files	li	3. **A Git repository:** add `.DS_Store` to Git’s global ignore file so it’s never committed again, and remove any already committed with `git rm --cached .DS_Store`.
X	ds-store-files	code	find /Volumes/MyDrive -name .DS_Store -type f -delete
X	ds-store-files	code	mkdir -p ~/.config/git && echo .DS_Store >> ~/.config/git/ignore
X	ds-store-files	p	Git uses `~/.config/git/ignore` as its global ignore file by default, unless `core.excludesFile` or `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` points somewhere else. For network shares, Apple documents a setting that stops Finder using `.DS_Store` files on SMB shares; log out and back in after running it:
X	ds-store-files	code	defaults write com.apple.desktopservices DSDontWriteNetworkStores -bool TRUE
X	ds-store-files	p	Apple presents it as a way to make browsing a share faster; folders there then list in plain alphanumeric order. Run it again with `FALSE` at the end to undo it.
X	ds-store-files	h	Sources
X	ds-store-files	src	Apple: Adjust SMB browsing behavior in macOS: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102064
X	ds-store-files	src	Wikipedia: .DS_Store: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/.DS_Store
X	ds-store-files	src	Git documentation: gitignore (the global ignore file): https://git-scm.com/docs/gitignore
X	dot-trashes	p	**Yes, by emptying the Trash.** `.Trashes` is where a drive keeps the things you’ve already thrown away from it, and a Recovered Files folder holds temporary files an app left behind. Emptying the Trash in Finder clears both.
X	dot-trashes	p	**What it costs you:** whatever is in the Trash is gone for good, so look first. Apple suggests checking a Recovered Files folder for anything useful before emptying.
X	dot-trashes	p	**Does it come back?** The hidden folders return the next time you trash something on that drive or an app saves to it. They’re empty and tiny until then.
X	dot-trashes	h	Measure it
X	dot-trashes	code	du -sh "/Volumes/<drive>/.Trashes" "/Volumes/<drive>/.TemporaryItems" 2>/dev/null
X	dot-trashes	h	How to clear it
X	dot-trashes	li	1. **Connect the drive** to the Mac that the files were trashed on, and log in as the same user.
X	dot-trashes	li	2. **Open the Trash** from the Dock and look through it. Drag out anything you still want.
X	dot-trashes	li	3. **For a Recovered Files folder**, open it. Apple’s advice is to drag any useful files out of the Trash, and to check with the app’s developer if you aren’t sure what they are.
X	dot-trashes	li	4. **Empty the Trash**: click Empty in the Trash window, or choose Finder › Empty Trash. Apple warns that emptied items are permanently deleted.
X	dot-trashes	li	5. **Eject the drive** in Finder before unplugging it.
X	dot-trashes	p	There’s no need to remove the `.Trashes` or `.TemporaryItems` folders themselves. Empty, they take no meaningful space, and macOS makes them again when needed. If a drive is used only with cameras or other devices and the hidden folders bother you, erasing the drive in Disk Utility, after copying your files off it, gives a clean start.
X	dot-trashes	p	Don’t delete inside `.TemporaryItems` while apps have documents open on that drive. A save in progress may be using it.
X	dot-trashes	h	Sources
X	dot-trashes	src	Apple: If the Trash contains recovered files on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mchlp2919/mac
X	dot-trashes	src	Apple: Delete files and folders on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mchlp1093/mac
X	dot-trashes	src	Apple Developer: FileManager url(for:in:appropriateFor:create:), temporary directories on a volume: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/foundation/filemanager/url(for:in:appropriatefor:create:)
X	dot-trashes	src	MacMost: How the Mac Trash handles external drive files: https://macmost.com/how-mac-trash-handles-external-drive-files.html
X	aerial-wallpapers	p	**Yes, the videos.** The aerial clips in `/Library/Application Support/com.apple.idleassetsd/Customer` are downloads, not part of macOS. Removing the video files loses nothing you made.
X	aerial-wallpapers	p	**What it costs you:** the moving wallpaper and screen saver fall back to a still picture until the clip is fetched again. Leave the database and the small files beside the videos alone.
X	aerial-wallpapers	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, for whichever aerials your wallpaper or screen saver still uses. To keep the folder small, pick a still wallpaper and a screen saver that isn’t an aerial, and avoid Shuffle Aerials.
X	aerial-wallpapers	h	Measure it
X	aerial-wallpapers	code	du -sh "/Library/Application Support/com.apple.idleassetsd"
X	aerial-wallpapers	code	du -sh "/Library/Application Support/com.apple.idleassetsd/Customer"/*
X	aerial-wallpapers	h	How to clear it
X	aerial-wallpapers	li	1. **Stop new downloads first.** Open System Settings › Wallpaper and choose a still picture or a colour. Then click Screen Saver and pick one that isn’t an aerial. If you want to keep one aerial, choose that single one and not Shuffle Aerials.
X	aerial-wallpapers	li	2. **Open the folder.** In Finder choose Go › Go to Folder and paste `/Library/Application Support/com.apple.idleassetsd/Customer`.
X	aerial-wallpapers	li	3. **Open the format folder that `du` showed as large** and move the video files in it to the Trash. Finder asks for an administrator’s password.
X	aerial-wallpapers	li	4. **Leave everything else**: `Aerial.sqlite`, `snapshots`, `entries.json` and the other small files. They are how macOS knows which aerials exist.
X	aerial-wallpapers	li	5. **Empty the Trash** to get the space back.
X	aerial-wallpapers	p	Don’t delete the whole `com.apple.idleassetsd` folder or its database to stop downloads. Changing the wallpaper and screen saver choice is the supported way.
