X	font-caches	p	**Yes**, with Apple’s tools. Font caches are databases macOS builds from the fonts you have installed, so resetting them deletes no fonts. It’s a repair for fonts that misbehave, not a way to free space.
X	font-caches	p	**What it costs you:** a restart or a log out, and some Font Book choices to redo: fonts you turned off can be back on, and fonts you activated from other folders may need activating again.
X	font-caches	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. macOS builds new databases from the fonts in the standard font folders after you log out or restart.
X	font-caches	h	Measure it
X	font-caches	code	du -sh "$(getconf DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR)com.apple.FontRegistry"
X	font-caches	h	How to clear it
X	font-caches	p	Start with the gentlest step that fixes the problem:
X	font-caches	li	1. **Check the fonts first.** In Font Book, select fonts and choose File › Validate Selection. Fonts that fail show in red, and you can remove them with Remove Checked. File › Resolve Duplicates deals with two copies of the same font.
X	font-caches	li	2. **Start up in safe mode.** Apple says safe mode clears some system caches, including font caches, which macOS creates again as needed. Start up in safe mode, then restart normally and look at the fonts again.
X	font-caches	li	3. **Reset your own font caches from Terminal.** Quit your apps, run `atsutil databases -removeUser`, then log out and back in, or restart.
X	font-caches	code	atsutil databases -removeUser
X	font-caches	p	Its manual says `-removeUser` removes the font databases for the active user only, which is enough for a problem in your own account; `-remove` clears the system’s databases as well. Older guides also tell you to run `atsutil server -shutdown` and `atsutil server -ping`. The `atsutil` in recent macOS has no `server` command (`atsutil help` lists what yours has); logging out or restarting does that job.
X	font-caches	h	Sources
X	font-caches	src	Apple: Start up your Mac in safe mode: https://support.apple.com/en-us/116946
X	font-caches	src	atsutil(8) man page: font registration system utility: https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/atsutil.8.html
X	font-caches	src	Apple: Install and validate fonts in Font Book on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/font-book/fntbk1000/mac
X	iphone-software-updates	p	**Yes.** An `.ipsw` file is a copy of iOS or iPadOS your Mac downloaded to update or restore a device. Once that’s done nothing needs it, and your iPhone backups aren’t in it.
X	iphone-software-updates	p	**What it costs you:** a fresh download the next time you update or restore a device from this Mac.
X	iphone-software-updates	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, when it’s needed: the Mac downloads the software again for the next update or restore.
