X	lost-and-found	p	**Yes, after checking it.** The folder holds files that a disk repair rescued because they had lost their place in the folder structure. macOS doesn’t use them from there.
X	lost-and-found	p	**What it costs you:** possibly the only surviving copy of a file that went missing when the disk was damaged. Open what’s inside before you decide.
X	lost-and-found	p	**Does it come back?** Only if a later repair finds more orphaned files. If that keeps happening, the drive itself needs attention.
X	lost-and-found	h	Measure it
X	lost-and-found	code	du -sh "/Volumes/<drive>/lost+found"
X	lost-and-found	code	file "/Volumes/<drive>/lost+found"/* | head -20
X	lost-and-found	h	How to clear it
X	lost-and-found	li	1. **Think back.** Did anything go missing from this drive around the time of a crash, a bad eject or a power cut? If so, it may be in here.
X	lost-and-found	li	2. **Identify the files** with the `file` command above, or select one in Finder and press Space for a preview.
X	lost-and-found	li	3. **Copy anything worth keeping** to a different disk, and give it a proper name and extension so its app opens it.
X	lost-and-found	li	4. **Follow Apple’s rule for damaged files:** if you can replace a file or create it again, delete it; if it contains information you need, open it and examine its data to make sure it hasn’t been corrupted.
X	lost-and-found	li	5. **Move `lost+found` to the Trash**, then empty the Trash. Finder may ask for an administrator’s password.
X	lost-and-found	p	Then check the drive’s health. Run First Aid again from Disk Utility. Apple’s guide says that if Disk Utility can’t repair the disk, or First Aid reports that the process failed, you should back up as much of your data as possible, then reformat the disk. More on reading the result: First Aid found corruption.
X	lost-and-found	h	Sources
X	lost-and-found	src	macOS manual page: fsck_hfs(8): https://keith.github.io/xcode-man-pages/fsck_hfs.8.html
X	lost-and-found	src	Apple: Repair a storage device in Disk Utility on Mac: https://support.apple.com/guide/disk-utility/repair-a-storage-device-dskutl1040/mac
X	macos-installers	p	**Yes, the installers.** An app named “Install macOS …” in Applications is a full macOS installer that was downloaded and never removed. It isn’t the macOS your Mac runs, and Apple lets you download it again whenever you need it.
X	macos-installers	p	**What it costs you:** nothing, unless you meant to upgrade with it soon or make a bootable USB installer from it. Keep it until you have.
X	macos-installers	p	**Does it come back?** Only if you download it again, from Software Update, the App Store or Apple’s download page.
X	macos-installers	h	How to clear it
X	macos-installers	li	1. Check which macOS you run: Apple menu › About This Mac.
X	macos-installers	li	2. An installer for that version or an older one has nothing left to do. Drag it to the Trash and empty the Trash. If Finder asks for an administrator’s name and password, an administrator account installed it.
X	macos-installers	li	3. An installer for a newer macOS you haven’t installed yet: keep it until you upgrade, or delete it and let Software Update download what it needs when you’re ready.
X	macos-installers	li	4. Want a bootable USB installer? Make it first with Apple’s `createinstallmedia` steps; Apple’s guide then says you can delete the installer from your Applications folder.
X	macos-installers	p	If the installer is open, quit it first. Never try to free space from the macOS part of your disk or anything under `/System`: that’s the system itself, and macOS protects it.
X	macos-installers	h	Sources
