X	relocated-items	p	**Usually yes,** once you’ve looked inside. Apple says the folder holds files that a macOS upgrade couldn’t move to their new locations. macOS itself doesn’t use them from there.
X	relocated-items	p	**What it costs you:** nothing, unless one of those files is a setting or document you still want. Read the PDF in the folder and check the rest before you delete.
X	relocated-items	p	**Does it come back?** Not by itself. A later macOS upgrade can make a new one if it again finds files it can’t place.
X	relocated-items	h	Measure it
X	relocated-items	code	du -sh "/Users/Shared/Relocated Items"
X	relocated-items	code	ls -R "/Users/Shared/Relocated Items" | head -50
X	relocated-items	h	How to clear it
X	relocated-items	li	1. **Open the folder** from the desktop alias and read the PDF that Apple puts in it.
X	relocated-items	li	2. **Look through the other files.** If you recognise something you set up yourself and still need, copy it somewhere in your home folder, such as Documents. Don’t try to put files back into system folders.
X	relocated-items	li	3. **Check that your Mac and your apps work** as they did before the upgrade. If something that used to work has stopped, keep the folder until you’ve sorted that out, since the old file may show what changed.
X	relocated-items	li	4. **Drag the Relocated Items folder** in `/Users/Shared` to the Trash. Finder may ask for an administrator’s password.
X	relocated-items	li	5. **Drag the alias on the desktop to the Trash** too, then empty the Trash.
X	relocated-items	p	Deleting only the desktop alias removes the shortcut and frees no space. The folder stays in `/Users/Shared` until you delete it there.
X	relocated-items	h	Sources
X	relocated-items	src	Apple: If a Relocated Items folder appears on your Mac after upgrading macOS: https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/a-relocated-items-folder-appears-mac-mchl8ae423a3/mac
X	downloads-folder	p	**Yes, whatever you no longer need.** Downloads is your folder: macOS and your apps don’t rely on what’s in it. Installers for apps you’ve already installed and zip files you’ve already unpacked are the easy wins.
X	downloads-folder	p	**What it costs you:** anything that turns out to be your only copy, like a ticket, a bank statement or a file someone sent you. Open those before they go.
X	downloads-folder	p	**Does it come back?** Not by itself. A deleted download returns only if you download it again, and not every link works twice.
X	downloads-folder	h	Measure it
X	downloads-folder	code	du -sh ~/Downloads
X	downloads-folder	code	du -sh ~/Downloads/* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20
X	downloads-folder	h	How to clear it
X	downloads-folder	li	1. In Finder, open Downloads, choose View › as List and click the Size column to put the biggest files first.
X	downloads-folder	li	2. Installers (`.dmg`, `.pkg`) for apps that are already in Applications can go. The maker’s site has a newer copy if you ever need one.
X	downloads-folder	li	3. A `.zip` next to a folder with the same name has been unpacked. Keep the folder and delete the zip, or the other way round.
X	downloads-folder	li	4. Open anything you can’t place before deleting it: boarding passes, invoices, scanned letters and contracts end up here too.
X	downloads-folder	li	5. Move what you want to keep to Documents or another folder. Select the rest, press ⌘⌫ to move it to the Trash, then empty the Trash.
X	downloads-folder	p	Delete the files, not the Downloads folder itself: browsers and apps expect it to be there.
X	downloads-folder	h	Sources
