X	preboot-volume	p	**No.** Preboot is one of the volumes macOS starts up from. It holds files used before the system is running, and the sealed disk images that carry Safari and other parts of macOS. Remove them and the Mac may not start.
X	preboot-volume	p	**What frees space instead:** nothing inside Preboot is yours to clear. Look at your own files, caches and snapshots, which is where reclaimable space is.
X	preboot-volume	p	**Does it come back?** macOS writes this volume itself during installs and updates. A reinstall of macOS from Recovery is the supported way to rebuild it.
X	preboot-volume	h	How to clear it
X	preboot-volume	p	Preboot shares the disk’s free space with your Data volume, so its gigabytes do count against you. But they are the working parts of macOS, the same as the system volume itself. There is no cache or leftover in there with a supported way to remove it, and guides that suggest deleting folders from Recovery or after turning off System Integrity Protection risk a Mac that stops at startup.
X	preboot-volume	li	1. **Keep macOS up to date.** Updates replace the cryptexes with new ones.
X	preboot-volume	li	2. **If you believe it is damaged or far larger than Disk Utility says is normal,** back up, then reinstall macOS from Recovery. Apple says reinstalling doesn’t remove your apps or personal data.
X	preboot-volume	li	3. **For space, look where you’re in charge:** what is taking up space, local Time Machine snapshots and old macOS installers.
X	preboot-volume	h	Sources
X	preboot-volume	src	Apple Platform Security: Signed system volume security: https://support.apple.com/guide/security/signed-system-volume-security-secd698747c9/web
X	preboot-volume	src	Apple: How to reinstall macOS: https://support.apple.com/en-us/102655
X	preboot-volume	src	Howard Oakley, The Eclectic Light Company: Boot volume layout and structure in macOS Sequoia: https://eclecticlight.co/2024/10/22/boot-volume-layout-and-structure-in-macos-sequoia/
