X	ios-device-support	p	**Yes**, with Xcode quit, and especially the folders for iOS versions none of your devices run any more. They’re copies of system symbols Xcode took from your iPhone or iPad so it could debug apps on it. Your apps, projects and archives aren’t in there.
X	ios-device-support	p	**What it costs you:** the next time you connect a device whose folder you deleted, Xcode copies its symbols again before it can debug on it, so that first connection takes longer.
X	ios-device-support	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, for each device you plug in again. Folders for iOS versions you no longer use simply don’t come back.
X	ios-device-support	h	Measure it
X	ios-device-support	code	du -sh ~/Library/Developer/Xcode/*DeviceSupport
X	ios-device-support	code	du -sh ~/Library/Developer/Xcode/"iOS DeviceSupport"/* | sort -h
X	ios-device-support	h	How to clear it
X	ios-device-support	li	1. Quit Xcode and disconnect your devices, so nothing is copying symbols while you delete.
X	ios-device-support	li	2. Open `~/Library/Developer/Xcode/iOS DeviceSupport` in Finder.
X	ios-device-support	li	3. Move the folders for iOS versions none of your devices run any more to the Trash. Keep the folders that match the devices you debug on now, so you don’t wait for them to be copied again. Then empty the Trash.
X	ios-device-support	p	Or clear everything inside it from Terminal, with Xcode quit:
X	ios-device-support	code	rm -rf ~/Library/Developer/Xcode/"iOS DeviceSupport"/*
X	ios-device-support	p	Clearing the whole folder is also one of Apple’s own fixes. When Xcode failed to copy symbols from devices, the Xcode 12.2 release notes gave this workaround: disconnect every device, delete `~/Library/Developer/Xcode/iOS DeviceSupport`, open the Devices and Simulators window, and reconnect each device with a USB cable so Xcode copies its symbols again.
