X	xcode-simulators	code	xcrun simctl runtime list
X	xcode-simulators	h	How to clear it
X	xcode-simulators	p	**Simulators for OS versions you’ve removed.** They can’t run any more, so they can all go at once. Quit Simulator, then:
X	xcode-simulators	code	xcrun simctl delete unavailable
X	xcode-simulators	p	**Simulators you don’t use.** Take the UDID from `xcrun simctl list devices` and delete just that one:
X	xcode-simulators	code	xcrun simctl delete <UDID>
X	xcode-simulators	p	**Runtimes you no longer target.** In Xcode, choose Xcode › Settings, click Components, click the info button next to the runtime and choose Delete. Apple’s support staff describe this as the intended way. From Terminal:
X	xcode-simulators	code	xcrun simctl runtime list
X	xcode-simulators	code	xcrun simctl runtime delete <runtime-id>
X	xcode-simulators	p	**SwiftUI preview simulators.** With Xcode quit, `xcrun simctl --set previews delete all` clears the preview device set; developers on Apple’s forums report previews working normally afterwards.
X	xcode-simulators	p	Avoid deleting folders inside `Devices` by hand. CoreSimulator keeps its own record of the devices it manages, and `simctl` removes the record and the folder together.
