X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	p	**Yes.** Both folders store results of earlier builds so the next one can skip work. Neither holds source code or anything you wrote.
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	p	**What it costs you:** one cold build. Turborepo runs every task again and Parcel rebuilds every file, where they would have reused cached results.
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, on the next `turbo run` or the next time Parcel starts.
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	h	Measure it
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	code	find ~/Projects \( -name .turbo -o -name .parcel-cache \) -type d -prune -exec du -sh {} + 2>/dev/null | sort -h
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	h	How to clear it
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	li	1. Stop the dev server, watcher or build that is running in the project. Both tools write to their cache while they work.
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	li	2. Remove the folder, giving its full path.
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	code	rm -rf "$HOME/Projects/my-monorepo/.turbo"
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	code	rm -rf "$HOME/Projects/my-site/.parcel-cache"
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	p	If you’re chasing a stale build and don’t need the space, there are flags for that and nothing has to be deleted. `turbo run build --force` runs the tasks again; Turborepo’s docs note this disables reading the cache, not writing, so fresh results are still stored. Parcel’s `--no-cache` likewise only disables reading: a `.parcel-cache` folder is still created.
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	h	Sources
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	src	Turborepo Docs: Caching: https://turborepo.dev/docs/crafting-your-repository/caching
X	turbo-and-parcel-cache	src	Parcel Docs: Development (caching): https://parceljs.org/features/development/
X	pycache	p	**Yes.** Every file in `__pycache__` is compiled from a `.py` file beside it, and Python compiles it again by itself. Nothing in there is the only copy of your code.
X	pycache	p	**What it costs you:** the next import of each module is a little slower while Python compiles it again. Code doesn’t run slower once it’s loaded.
X	pycache	p	**Does it come back?** Yes, as soon as the code runs again, unless you tell Python not to write these files.
X	pycache	h	How to clear it
X	pycache	p	From the top of a project, this removes every `__pycache__` folder under it and nothing else:
X	pycache	code	cd ~/Projects/my-app
X	pycache	code	find . -type d -name __pycache__ -prune -exec rm -rf {} +
X	pycache	p	Run it inside the project folder, never from your home folder or `/`, and run the `find` part on its own first if you want to see the list. Python programs already running carry on; their modules are loaded.
X	pycache	h3	Stopping them from appearing
X	pycache	li	- `python -B`, or `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1` in your environment, tells Python not to write `.pyc` files on import. Imports then compile every time.
X	pycache	li	- `PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX`, in Python 3.8 and later, keeps the files but writes them to a mirror tree in one folder instead of `__pycache__` folders in your code.
X	pycache	code	export PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX="$HOME/Library/Caches/pycache"
X	pycache	h	Sources
X	pycache	src	Python tutorial: compiled Python files: https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/modules.html#compiled-python-files
X	pycache	src	Python docs: command line and environment (-B, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE, PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX): https://docs.python.org/3/using/cmdline.html
X	cocoapods-pods-folder	p	**Yes**, if the project has its `Podfile` and `Podfile.lock`. `pod install` reads them and puts the same pod versions back.
X	cocoapods-pods-folder	p	**What it costs you:** the project won’t build until `pod install` has run, and that needs the network unless the pods are still in CocoaPods’ own cache.
X	cocoapods-pods-folder	p	**Does it come back?** Not by itself. You run `pod install` in the project folder.
X	cocoapods-pods-folder	h	Measure it
X	cocoapods-pods-folder	code	du -sh ~/Projects/MyApp/Pods
X	cocoapods-pods-folder	code	find ~/Projects -type d -name Pods -prune -exec du -sh {} + 2>/dev/null | sort -h
X	cocoapods-pods-folder	h	How to clear it
