X	npm-cache	p	**Yes.** `~/.npm` only holds copies of packages npm has already downloaded, plus logs and tools fetched by `npx`. Your projects, their `node_modules` and your global packages aren’t in it.
X	npm-cache	p	**What it costs you:** the next install of each package downloads it again, so that install needs the network and takes longer.
X	npm-cache	p	**Does it come back?** Yes. npm adds to it with every install and never removes anything on its own.
X	npm-cache	h	Measure it
X	npm-cache	code	du -sh ~/.npm
X	npm-cache	code	du -sh ~/.npm/_cacache ~/.npm/_npx ~/.npm/_logs
X	npm-cache	h	How to clear it
X	npm-cache	li	1. Let any `npm install` that’s running finish.
X	npm-cache	li	2. For a trim, run `npm cache verify`. It checks the cache, garbage-collects data nothing points to any more, and reports what it removed. Sometimes that’s enough.
X	npm-cache	li	3. To empty it, run `npm cache clean --force`. npm asks for `--force` on purpose: its docs say clearing should never be needed for any reason other than getting disk space back.
X	npm-cache	code	npm cache verify
X	npm-cache	code	npm cache clean --force
X	npm-cache	p	The `_npx` folder can go as well; `npx` downloads a tool again the next time you run it. With npm not running:
X	npm-cache	code	rm -rf ~/.npm/_npx/*
X	npm-cache	p	Leave `~/.npmrc` alone. It’s a separate file in your home folder with your npm settings and any registry tokens you’ve logged in with.
